  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nHigh clouds have started to stream in ahead of an ongoing area of \nshowers and storms in central North Dakota. These showers will \ncontinue to diminish as they arrive in western Minnesota by this \nevening. After sunset, a strengthening LLJ and the main \nshortwave will arrive and shower and thunderstorm coverage will \nincrease during the early morning hours. QPF wise, both \ndeterministic models and ensembles can't see to agree on where \nthe heaviest axis of precip will fall. There have been dramatic \nshifts in the precip placement from not only model to model but \nrun to run as well. A wide footprint of around a quarter inch \nseems reasonable with localized amounts near or just over a half\ninch somewhere across central Minnesota. SPC did pull the \nSWODY1 Marginal Risk further east across western and southern \nMinnesota for the potential that storms grow upscale with a \ndamaging wind risk early tomorrow morning. Otherwise, \ninstability is lacking for the risk of more widespread severe \nweather.\n\nRain will end from west to east across far western Minnesota by mid \nmorning with showers hanging on into the afternoon for western \nWisconsin. Clouds will be slower to clear, but some afternoon sun is \npossible for western and southern Minnesota. Southerly winds turn \neasterly by the evening with a frontal boundary stalling out \nsomewhere across southern Minnesota. Storms are possible again \nSunday afternoon, and these could pose a higher risk for severe \nweather given a much more favorable environment. This front will \nstick around into early next week with daily chances of \nshowers/thunderstorms, but any widespread, prolonged beneficial \nrainfall does not seem very likely at this time. Additionally, highs \nwill be in the mid to upper 80s and lows in the upper 60s through at \nleast the middle of next week, which is about 10 degrees above \nnormal. This, combined with Tds in the mid to upper 60s/near 70 \nmeans that summer-like weather will hang on as we head into \nmeteorological Fall.",
